1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,500
This is HearGraph, my solo entry for AccessED Hacks. I'm Dylan Rolfe.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:12,500
The barrier. A student who is blind or has low vision
gets a physics worksheet.

3
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:18,500
The graph is a picture. A screen reader just says:
"graph of velocity, image."

4
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:25,500
The student can't read a value or find where the line
crosses zero without someone's help.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:33,500
The change. The teacher usually has the numbers behind the graph.
In HearGraph, they paste them in.

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,000
A title, a small table, and Create link.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:49,500
The data is stored inside the link itself.
No account, no upload, nothing saved on a server.

8
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,500
The student opens the link.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:04,500
First, a short description in plain language: where the line starts,
how it changes, and where it reaches zero.

10
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Then the graph itself. One Tab stop.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:21,500
The arrow keys move point by point,
and every point is announced with its units.

12
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The question was: when does the cart stop?
Press Z to jump to the zero crossing.

13
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,500
[Screen reader] "Time 4 seconds, velocity 0 metres per second,
crossing zero."

14
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Space plays the whole graph as sound. Pitch follows the value.

15
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:49,500
[Tones fall in pitch. A click marks zero.
Two low ticks mark the lowest value.]

16
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
The same numbers are in a real data table.

17
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:04,500
Then practice questions, with no timer,
and feedback that explains the answer.

18
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
For low vision there's high contrast, large text,
and a ringed, labelled focus point.

19
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,500
After the first visit it works offline.

20
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:24,000
What it doesn't do. The teacher has to supply the numbers.
It can't read a picture.

21
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Sound is an extra, not a replacement for tactile graphics
or a specialist teacher.

22
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
I tested it myself with automated checks and a screen reader.
It has not been tested with blind or low-vision students.

23
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:45,500
I built it during the event with an AI coding agent.
Details and credits are in the README. Thanks for watching.
